WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Scoring a model: negative log-likelihood.

2
00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:10.136
The likelihood of the dataset is the product of the probabilities the model assigns to every bigram that actually occurs.

3
00:00:10.416 --> 00:00:18.203
That product is a tiny number (thousands of factors below 1), so you work with its logarithm, which turns the product into a sum.

4
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:25.523
Flip the sign so that lower is better, and divide by the number of bigrams so the loss doesn’t depend on dataset size.

5
00:00:25.803 --> 00:00:34.464
That’s the average negative log-likelihood (NLL), which for classification is the same quantity as cross-entropy between the data and the model.

6
00:00:34.744 --> 00:00:36.536
Anchor it with two reference points.

7
00:00:36.816 --> 00:00:43.664
A model that assigns every token probability 1/27 scores log 27, about 3.

8
00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:46.013
296, on any data.

9
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:48.064
A perfect model scores 0.

10
00:00:48.344 --> 00:00:51.629
For this lesson, the counting model was fit to all of names.

11
00:00:51.909 --> 00:00:58.267
txt with the method in the video, and its average NLL on that same data comes out at about 2.

12
00:00:58.547 --> 00:00:59.997
454.

13
00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:05.824
So the bigram structure is real (it beats uniform comfortably), and there’s lots left to learn.

14
00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:18.221
Exponentiate the NLL and you get perplexity, the effective number of equally likely choices the model is torn between at each step: about 27 for the uniform model, about 11.

15
00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:19.909
6 for the bigram model.

16
00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:26.376
You’ll see perplexity in older papers and NLL (often called “loss”) in nearly everything recent.
